\subsection{2-factor TFP estimation}

I also assume the orthogonality of capital and labour shocks
on a cross-sectional basis.

\begin{align}
    \notag
    x_{i,t} &=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        k_{i,t} &   l_{i,t}
    \end{pmatrix}
    \\
    \label{eq:static-k-pim}
    K_{i,t} &= I_{i,t} + dK_{i,t-1}
    \\
    \label{eq:static-inv-information}
    I_{i,t} &= I_{i,t}^* + e^I_t
    \\
    \label{eq:static-l-information}
    L_{i,t} &= L_{i,t}^* + e^L_t
    \\
    \mathbb E[\epsilon_{i,t}|\Omega_{t-1}] &= 0 
    \\ \notag
    \varepsilon_{i,t} &\in \Omega_t
    \\ \notag
    &\implies K_{i,t} \propto \epsilon_{t-1}
    \\ \notag
    &\implies L_{i,t} \propto \epsilon_{t}
    \\
    \mathbb E[\varepsilon_{i,t} \vert k_{i,t}] &= 0,\ 
    \mathbb E[\varepsilon_{i,t} \vert l_{i,t}] = 0
\end{align}
\par
This helpfully means $z_{i,t}$ can actually be calculated directly
with no further econometric issues as the residual of the panel 

For convenience, I initially conduct the 2-factor estimation
deriving the residual $z_{i,t}$ to decompose further in a second stage.
This step is additionally helpful for validating the relationships about
the derived aggregates from my data.

\begin{align}
    \label{eqn:tfp2factor}
    y_{i,t} &= \alpha_i + \gamma_t + \beta_0
                + \beta_1 k_{i,t}
                + \beta_2 l_{i,t}
                + u_{i,t}
    \\ \notag
    u_{i,t} &= \delta_0 \sum_{j\neq i} w_{ij}z_{j,t} + \varepsilon_{i,t}
    \\
    z_{i,t} &= \alpha_i + \gamma_t + \beta_0 + u_{i,t}
    \\
    \hat z_{i,t} &= y_{i,t} - \hat\beta_1k_{i,t} - \hat\beta_2l_{i,t}
\end{align}
%
